# SourceBridge environment configuration
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in your key. `.env.local` is gitignored.

# At least one provider key is required. Both are free.
#
# With only GROQ_API_KEY the app generates every format but cannot read an
# image or video source, and cannot speak video narration: those need Gemini.
# With only GEMINI_API_KEY everything works, more slowly.

# Google Gemini API key -- get one free at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Read ONLY on the server. It is never sent to the browser.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Groq API key -- get one free at https://console.groq.com/keys
# Tried before Gemini for text, because it answers in about a second where the
# Gemini free tier was measured taking tens of seconds, and because its
# allowance is separate: a spent Gemini quota no longer stops the app.
GROQ_API_KEY=

# Primary model. Model availability changes over time; if you see a "no longer
# available" error, set this to a current model name.
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-flash-lite-latest

# Optional. Comma-separated models tried when the primary is overloaded.
# Free-tier capacity fluctuates, so rotating recovers faster than waiting.
# Defaults to: gemini-3.8-flash,gemini-3.6-flash,gemini-3.5-flash,
#              gemini-3.1-flash-lite,gemma-4-31b-it,gemma-4-26b-a4b-it
# Ordered by measurement against the real schemas. The Gemma entries stay in
# because they draw on a separate allowance and keep the app working once the
# Gemini daily quotas are spent, but they sit last: one took three minutes and
# then returned invalid JSON.
# GEMINI_FALLBACK_MODELS=gemini-3.8-flash,gemini-3.6-flash,gemini-3.5-flash

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: the OpenAI-compatible provider
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint works here -- OpenRouter, Cerebras and GitHub
# Models all speak the same shape. Point these at one to switch provider
# without a code change.
# GROQ_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
# GROQ_LABEL=Groq

# Comma-separated models, tried in order.
# Defaults to: openai/gpt-oss-120b,openai/gpt-oss-20b
# Qwen is available on Groq but is not in the default chain: across three runs
# it reproduced only half the source figures twice. Losing a figure is the one
# failure this application exists to prevent.
# GROQ_MODELS=openai/gpt-oss-120b,openai/gpt-oss-20b

# Hidden reasoning budget, where the model supports it: low, medium or high.
# Reasoning tokens count against the per-minute allowance, and low was measured
# using about half the tokens for the same output quality. Defaults to low.
# GROQ_REASONING_EFFORT=low

# How long a stated rate-limit delay may be before the app stops waiting it out
# and moves to another model instead. Defaults to 12000.
# PROVIDER_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_MS=12000

# Raster width for video frames, independent of the layout: the scene cards are
# authored in a 1920x1080 space and rasterised to whatever is asked for, so a
# lower value changes resolution and nothing about the design.
#
# Memory, not time, is what limits a render on a small host. On a 512 MB
# container, three scenes rendered at 1920 and four did not. 1280 costs 55%
# fewer pixels per frame. Leave unset for full HD where there is room.
#
# The Docker image sets this to 1280 already, because a container is where the
# limit bites and a typical package is five to seven scenes. Set it to 1920
# there to opt back into full HD.
# VIDEO_RENDER_WIDTH=1280

# Optional. Comma-separated text-to-speech models, tried in order, used only by
# the rendered MP4 export. Speech is metered per model per day, and every scene
# costs one call, so the chain width decides how many videos a day a free key
# can render.
# Defaults to: gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts,gemini-3.8-flash-tts,
#              gemini-3.8-flash-lite-tts,gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview
# The Pro speech model is left out: it answers with a billing error on a free key.
# GEMINI_TTS_MODELS=gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts,gemini-3.8-flash-tts

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: where video narration comes from
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Speech was the one thing here with a hard daily ceiling. The provider meters
# it per project, per model, per DAY -- the refusal names the quota as
# GenerateRequestsPerDayPerProjectPerModel-FreeTier -- and every scene costs a
# request, so one six-scene video could spend the whole free allowance. More
# keys on the SAME Google project do not help, because the count is against the
# project rather than the key; keys from different projects do.
#
# So narration is no longer tied to one provider:
#
#   auto    the cloud voice while it lasts, then a local engine (default)
#   local   skip the cloud entirely -- no quota at all, and faster, since
#           nothing waits on a network
#   gemini  the cloud voice only; the render fails once it is spent
#   espeak  a local engine only
#
# The Docker image installs eSpeak NG, so video rendering keeps working with no
# speech key at all and no daily limit. It sounds synthetic, which is why the
# cloud voice is still preferred when available.
# VIDEO_TTS_ENGINE=auto

# Piper gives a neural local voice -- close to the cloud one, still no quota.
# It needs a voice model (tens of MB) chosen for your language; download one
# from the Piper releases and point at the .onnx file.
# PIPER_VOICE=/voices/en_GB-alba-medium.onnx
# PIPER_PATH=/usr/local/bin/piper

# eSpeak NG tuning. Any espeak-ng voice name works; the rate is words a minute.
# ESPEAK_VOICE=en-gb
# ESPEAK_WPM=160
# ESPEAK_PATH=/usr/bin/espeak-ng

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: protecting a public deployment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How many videos one visitor may render per hour. There are no accounts, so a
# public URL is open to anyone with the link, and rendering is by far the most
# expensive thing here. Defaults to 3. Set to 0 to turn the limit off, which is
# reasonable once speech runs locally and the only cost is CPU.
# VIDEO_RENDERS_PER_HOUR=3

# Optional. How many scenes are spoken at once during an MP4 render.
# Speech is essentially the whole render: measured on six scenes, 33s to speak
# them one after another against under 2s to draw every frame and under 1s to
# encode. Each call waits on the provider rather than on the machine, so they
# overlap well. Defaults to 4; the floor is one call, so beyond the scene count
# there is nothing further to gain. Raise it only if the per-minute allowance
# is generous, since a burst that trips a rate limit costs more than it saves.
# VIDEO_TTS_CONCURRENCY=4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: fonts for the rendered MP4
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Frames are drawn by resvg, which renders text ONLY with a font it can find.
# A minimal container image ships none at all, and resvg does not warn or
# substitute -- it draws no glyphs, so every slide comes out blank with the
# narration playing over it. The Docker image installs the Noto families for
# exactly this reason, and the renderer now refuses rather than producing a
# silent blank video. If you self-host without the Dockerfile, install a font:
#
#   apk add font-noto            # Alpine
#   apt install fonts-noto-core  # Debian / Ubuntu
#
# Family used when the stack in the frame resolves to nothing. Defaults to
# "Noto Sans".
# VIDEO_FONT_FAMILY=Noto Sans

# A directory of font files to load in addition to the system ones.
# VIDEO_FONT_DIR=/usr/share/fonts/truetype/custom

# Set to 0 to ignore system fonts entirely. With VIDEO_FONT_DIR this pins
# rendering to exactly the fonts you supply, so frames are identical on every
# host -- system fonts differ between machines, and the same text can wrap
# differently as a result.
# VIDEO_FONT_LOAD_SYSTEM=1

# Optional. Absolute path to ffmpeg, used by the rendered MP4 export.
# Leave unset to use ffmpeg from PATH. Without ffmpeg the app still offers the
# video package (.zip); only the rendered .mp4 is unavailable.
# FFMPEG_PATH=C:/path/to/ffmpeg.exe
